document "How To Play"
description "A complete beginner's guide to Minecraft Bedrock Edition."

topic "Getting Started"
    description "Learn what Minecraft is, choose a game mode, create a world, and learn the basic controls."

    section "What Is Minecraft?"
        text
Minecraft is a sandbox game where you can explore, gather resources, craft items, build structures, fight hostile creatures, and create your own adventures.

There is no single required way to play Minecraft. You can focus on survival, building, exploration, automation, or any combination of them.
        end
    end

    section "Game Modes"
        subsection "Survival"
            text
Survival Mode requires you to gather resources, manage health and hunger, craft equipment, build shelter, and survive the dangers of the world.
            end
        end

        subsection "Creative"
            text
Creative Mode gives you access to blocks and items without requiring you to gather them normally.

You can fly, build freely, and focus on designing structures and testing ideas.
            end
        end

        subsection "Adventure"
            text
Adventure Mode is designed for custom maps and experiences where the world creator can control how players interact with blocks and items.
            end
        end

        subsection "Spectator"
            text
Spectator Mode allows you to observe the world without normally interacting with it.

Availability and behavior can depend on the Minecraft Bedrock Edition version and world settings.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Creating A World"
        subsection "World Name"
            text
Choose a name for your world.

The name is used to identify the world in your world list.
            end
        end

        subsection "Game Mode"
            text
Choose the game mode you want to use.

Survival is recommended when you want the traditional Minecraft progression experience.
            end
        end

        subsection "Difficulty"
            text
Choose the difficulty that matches the experience you want.

Difficulty affects gameplay elements such as hostile mobs and survival conditions.
            end

            tip
If you are learning Minecraft for the first time, you can start with an easier difficulty while you learn the basic mechanics.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Controls"
        text
Minecraft has different controls depending on whether you are playing with a keyboard and mouse, controller, or touchscreen device.

For the complete controls reference, see the Controls guide.
        end

        link "Open Controls" "Controls.html"
    end
end

topic "Starting Survival"
    description "Learn what to do during your first few minutes in a new Survival world."

    section "The First Few Minutes"
        subsection "Gather Wood"
            text
Find a tree and collect logs.

Wood is one of the most important early resources because it can be turned into planks, sticks, crafting tables, tools, and other useful items.
            end
        end

        subsection "Make A Crafting Table"
            text
Turn your logs into wooden planks.

Use four wooden planks to craft a crafting table.

The crafting table provides access to a larger crafting grid for creating tools and other items.
            end
        end

        subsection "Craft Basic Tools"
            text
Use your crafting table to make basic tools.

A wooden pickaxe is especially important because it allows you to collect stone and progress to stronger tools.
            end
        end

        subsection "Find Food"
            text
Find a reliable food source.

Food can restore hunger and help you survive while exploring and gathering resources.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Your First Shelter"
        subsection "Choose A Location"
            text
Choose a location that is reasonably safe and gives you enough space to build.

Being near useful resources, trees, water, and open land can make your early game easier.
            end
        end

        subsection "Build The Shelter"
            text
Build a simple shelter using materials that are easy to obtain.

Make sure it has walls, a roof, an entrance, and enough room for basic equipment.
            end

            tip
Your first shelter does not need to be impressive. The main goal is to give you a safe place to spend the night and store your belongings.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Surviving The Night"
        text
Hostile mobs can become a major danger at night.

Stay inside your shelter, light the area around you, or travel somewhere safe.

A bed can be used to sleep when conditions allow and can also set your respawn point.
        end
    end
end

topic "Resources And Equipment"
    description "Learn how to gather resources, craft equipment, manage food, and organize your items."

    section "Mining"
        subsection "Useful Early Resources"
            text
Important early resources include stone, coal, iron, and other ores.

Stone allows you to upgrade from wooden tools.

Coal can be used for torches and fuel.

Iron can be used to create stronger tools, weapons, armor, buckets, and many other items.
            end
        end

        subsection "Mining Safety"
            text
Always be careful when mining.

Watch for hostile mobs, lava, falls, and other hazards.

Bring enough tools, food, torches, and storage space for the mining trip.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Crafting"
        text
Crafting allows you to turn collected resources into useful items.

Use the crafting interface or crafting table to create tools, weapons, armor, blocks, food, and other items.
        end
    end

    section "Tools"
        text
Tools allow you to gather resources more efficiently.

Common tools include pickaxes, axes, shovels, hoes, and shears.

Different materials have different levels of durability and effectiveness.
        end
    end

    section "Food And Hunger"
        subsection "Early Food Sources"
            text
Early food sources can include crops, animals, and other food items that can be found or produced in the world.

Keep a supply of food with you when exploring and mining.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Armor"
        text
Armor protects you from many forms of damage.

As you progress, you can craft stronger armor from better materials.

Equip armor from your inventory to benefit from its protection.
        end
    end

    section "Inventory Management"
        text
Keep your inventory organized so you can quickly find important items.

Group similar items together, keep frequently used items in your hotbar, and avoid carrying unnecessary items during long trips.
        end
    end

    section "Storage"
        text
Use chests and other storage blocks to keep resources organized.

Create separate storage areas for categories such as building blocks, ores, tools, food, and valuables.
        end
    end
end

topic "Exploration"
    description "Learn how to travel through the world, use coordinates, and find useful locations."

    section "Exploring"
        text
Minecraft worlds contain many different environments, structures, resources, and points of interest.

Explore carefully and keep track of where you are going so you can return to important locations.
        end

        subsection "Bring These Items"
            list
Food
Tools
Weapons
Armor
Torches
Building blocks
A bed
A map or other navigation aid when useful
            end
        end
    end

    section "Coordinates"
        subsection "Using Coordinates"
            text
Coordinates show your position in the Minecraft world.

The three coordinates represent your position along the X, Y, and Z axes.

Use coordinates to record the locations of important places such as your base, villages, mines, and portals.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Villages"
        text
Villages contain buildings, villagers, and useful resources.

Villagers can provide trading opportunities and can become an important part of a developed survival world.
        end

        link "Open Villagers Guide" "Villagers.html"
    end
end

topic "Building And Automation"
    description "Learn the basics of building, redstone, and farms."

    section "Building"
        subsection "Basic Building Principles"
            text
Start by choosing a building location and planning the shape of the structure.

Think about the foundation, walls, entrances, windows, roof, interior space, and surrounding area.

Use different blocks, shapes, heights, and details to make structures more interesting.
            end
        end
    end

    section "Redstone"
        text
Redstone allows you to create mechanisms and automated systems.

You can use redstone components to build doors, switches, farms, transportation systems, storage systems, and many other machines.
        end

        link "Open Redstone Guide" "Redstone.html"
    end

    section "Farms"
        text
Farms provide renewable supplies of resources.

Farms can range from simple manual crop farms to large automated systems using villagers, redstone, water, and other mechanics.
        end

        link "Open Farms Guide" "Farms.html"
    end
end

topic "Advanced Progression"
    description "Continue progressing after establishing a stable survival base."

    section "Enchanting"
        text
Enchanting allows you to improve equipment with special abilities.

An enchanting setup can become an important part of progressing toward stronger equipment and more advanced survival activities.
        end

        link "Open Enchanting Guide" "Enchanting.html"
    end

    section "The Nether"
        subsection "Before Entering"
            text
The Nether is a dangerous dimension containing unique environments, structures, mobs, and resources.

Before entering, bring useful equipment, food, blocks, and other supplies.

Make sure you can find your way back to your Nether portal.
            end
        end

        link "Open Dimensions Guide" "Dimensions.html"
    end

    section "The End"
        text
The End is another dimension containing unique terrain and structures.

Reaching The End is an important part of Minecraft's progression and requires preparation for a major challenge.
        end

        link "Open Dimensions Guide" "Dimensions.html"
    end

    section "Progression"
        text
Minecraft progression is open-ended.

After establishing your first shelter, continue improving your equipment, exploring new areas, gathering rarer resources, building larger projects, creating farms, and preparing for advanced challenges.

You can also focus on large building projects, redstone machines, exploration, or any other goals you create for yourself.
        end
    end
end

topic "Managing Your World"
    description "Learn how to handle death, save your progress, and decide what to do next."

    section "Death"
        text
When you die, you may drop items depending on the world's settings.

If possible, return to the location where you died and recover your belongings.

Keeping valuable items organized and storing important resources can reduce the impact of death.
        end
    end

    section "Saving And Leaving"
        text
Minecraft automatically saves your world as you play.

When you are finished playing, leave the world through the game's normal menu so your progress can be saved properly.
        end
    end

    section "What To Do Next"
        text
Once you have a stable base, food supply, equipment, and storage, choose your next goal.

You could improve your base, build a large project, explore new areas, create farms, work with villagers, mine for valuable resources, learn redstone, or prepare for the Nether and The End.

Minecraft does not require you to follow one fixed path. Your world can develop around whatever goals you decide to pursue.
        end
    end
end